#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749} {:answer/id #uuid "323a1bfd-c415-5a52-8f82-c95d150e0d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=10,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b95dc2-7fee-5f7c-b4ec-8675b76cbf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441601} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef271c-da7e-55bf-8af4-8d0b76cc331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441603} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6427-b31b-5669-b745-810c5329aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441605} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bbcaca-d001-596d-9fe9-529dd184cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441607} {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c819006-1c8a-567c-839e-c05698400658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4082a24-9ee0-5ed5-bf60-7857736d7c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126871" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a479bb-7541-5424-a117-bc163a220ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441596} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb606cd-8859-52ad-923b-4174c43b9bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441599} {:answer/id #uuid "bab36efc-1119-58ba-bc4b-a7d41daed2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441602} {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69b33-186c-5c7d-b495-4a5c8ed20698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ffc2c7-e514-54e6-8c7b-600c1de04088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b19a79-b263-5685-8630-5024a89d98bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-126893" :question/problem "![](images/df/df52e2add706a17bf93236cdaa04582932b6c799d4a88a3e194a3a3788c816ff.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+7x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=7x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc93cb5-dc56-5ce8-9e29-5d04cbab18f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441593} {:answer/id #uuid "590d73f3-e82d-557d-94b6-037ec0668422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441594} {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} {:answer/id #uuid "753d4b97-5af5-5ac8-b2eb-b98600ccb71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441598} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4fff1-a46e-50d1-90e7-531220bb1a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e8d331-dfbd-5435-859b-6678685b6ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e02e690-e43f-5ecb-8751-7e2242b3bc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-126936" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d35daa15e11899592683f2f3215506cc0337358be843780dbf1190c0e38e2c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441591} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81656c-ec2b-52fd-99b0-096af9bbcd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441597} {:answer/id #uuid "e220a614-c017-51de-896b-5544add5e4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441606} {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608} :answer-field/id #uuid "25134893-42bb-5ae1-bdf7-77b092bdf96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "311f57fc-3b6c-595d-a8b9-91e8d4d5d656" :question/math-academy-id "q-126947" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{8}{3}))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
